<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <RootNamespace>OpenVSA.Ui.Tests</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    The render core is deliberately free of WPF types, which is what lets REQ-UI-010's acceptance
    criterion - set four colours, render, sample the frame - run headlessly here rather than
    needing a window and a message pump. UseWPF is still required to reference the project.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <UseWPF>true</UseWPF>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\OpenVSA.Ui\OpenVSA.Ui.csproj" />

    <!--
      The synthetic modulated source of the display group. REQ-UI-050, REQ-UI-051 and REQ-UI-052
      are all worded against a signal whose symbols and symbol clock are known, so the tests that
      check them need the generator that produces one. A test-only reference to a test-only
      assembly; nothing in src\OpenVSA.Ui references it, and the layering test still holds.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\OpenVSA.TestHarness\OpenVSA.TestHarness.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    REQ-ARC-003's criterion end to end: "a new personality assembly dropped into Personalities\ is
    discovered on next launch, appears in the measurement-type selector, and runs - with no rebuild
    of the host".

    The example is built BEFORE these tests and deliberately NOT referenced. ReferenceOutputAssembly
    is false, so nothing here compiles against it and the shell cannot have been built with any
    knowledge of it; the file is then copied into Personalities\ beside the test host, which is the
    folder PersonalityRegistry.CreateDefault probes. A shell constructed afterwards finds it the
    same way a launched application would.

    Referencing it instead would put the type in the test's own output and prove only that
    reflection can find something the compiler already loaded.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\OpenVSA.Personality.Example\OpenVSA.Personality.Example.csproj">
      <ReferenceOutputAssembly>false</ReferenceOutputAssembly>
    </ProjectReference>
  </ItemGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OpenVsaExamplePersonality>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\OpenVSA.Personality.Example\bin\$(Configuration)\$(TargetFramework)\OpenVSA.Personality.Example.dll</OpenVsaExamplePersonality>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <Target Name="DeployExamplePersonality" AfterTargets="Build"
          Condition="Exists('$(OpenVsaExamplePersonality)')">
    <Copy SourceFiles="$(OpenVsaExamplePersonality)"
          DestinationFolder="$(OutDir)Personalities"
          SkipUnchangedFiles="true" />
  </Target>

  <!--
    REQ-TST-008's smoke suite drives the settings pane, and the pane is disabled until a front end
    has declared its capabilities (REQ-HAL-002). So the simulator has to be there to be selected.

    Deployed exactly as the shell deploys it, and for the same reason: ReferenceOutputAssembly is
    false, so nothing here compiles against OpenVSA.Hal.Sim and REQ-ARC-001's prohibition still
    holds - the shell under test cannot have been built with any knowledge of it. The file is copied
    into the FrontEnds\ subdirectory that FrontEndRegistry.CreateDefault probes, so a shell built on
    the test thread discovers it the way a launched application would.

    Referencing it instead would put the type in the test's own output and prove only that
    Activator can create something the compiler already loaded.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\OpenVSA.Hal.Sim\OpenVSA.Hal.Sim.csproj"
                      ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" Private="false" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OpenVsaSimulatedFrontEnd>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\..\src\OpenVSA.Hal.Sim\bin\$(Configuration)\$(TargetFramework)\OpenVSA.Hal.Sim.dll</OpenVsaSimulatedFrontEnd>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <Target Name="DeploySimulatedFrontEnd" AfterTargets="Build"
          Condition="Exists('$(OpenVsaSimulatedFrontEnd)')">
    <Copy SourceFiles="$(OpenVsaSimulatedFrontEnd)"
          DestinationFolder="$(OutDir)FrontEnds"
          SkipUnchangedFiles="true" />
  </Target>

</Project>
